research/A07_JOBS.md:3:VERDICT: A07 done; traces PM100 (180,310 completed jobs of 231,238), F-DATA/Fugaku (2,998,737 completed of 3,399,278; months 21_04, 23_01, 23_04, 23_07, 23_10), KIT FH2 (114,355 completed of 114,355; every SWF status is 1, so no TIMEOUT can be identified); median r_act/r_req 0.028 (PM100), 0.232 (F-DATA), 0.148 (KIT FH2); NLR Kestrel NOT obtained (HTTP 503 at 13:48 IST and again at 15:04 IST); see USER STEPS NEEDED.
research/A07_JOBS.md:8:- `r_req_h`, `r_act_h` in hours; `nodes` = allocated nodes (falls back to requested if allocated is 0).
research/A07_JOBS.md:9:- `state` mapping: COMPLETED, TIMEOUT, CANCELLED, FAILED stay; PM100 OUT_OF_MEMORY (1,011) and NODE_FAIL (170) are mapped to FAILED; anything else OTHER (none occurred). SWF status 1 = COMPLETED, 0 = FAILED, 5 = CANCELLED, other = OTHER. F-DATA `exit state`: completed / failed only.
research/A07_JOBS.md:11:- `r_pred_h` and `n_hist` exactly as specified: the user's jobs with END strictly before this job's submit and state COMPLETED or TIMEOUT; mean `r_act_h` of the two most recent by end time (one job: that job's value); none: `r_req_h`; then capped at `r_req_h`. `n_hist` = min(number of such jobs, 2). Implemented with sorted keys and `searchsorted` (vectorised). Ties in end time break by row order (rows sorted by submit time).
research/A07_JOBS.md:12:- Leakage check: for every trace, 1,000 random jobs were recomputed naively (pandas filter on user, `end < submit`, state, last two by end) and compared with the vectorised result; the assertion on `n_hist`, `r_pred_h` and on end < submit passed for all three traces (it runs inside `finalise()`, so any rerun re-asserts).
research/A07_JOBS.md:13:- No row was dropped: validity filter (submit present, `r_req_h` > 0, `r_act_h` >= 0 and finite, nodes >= 1) removed 0 rows in each trace. Jobs with `r_act_h` = 0 are kept (PM100 0.03 %, others none); the simulator should filter them if it needs a positive length.
research/A07_JOBS.md:20:Power: `node_power_consumption` is a list of 20 s samples of whole-job power in W (sum over all the job's nodes and both power supplies). Source: the field table (https://github.com/francescoantici/PM100-data/blob/main/documentation/job_features.md) says only "Power consumption of the job, recorded at Node level"; the generating script https://raw.githubusercontent.com/francescoantici/PM100-data/main/extract_data.py (`extract_job_power`) selects the job's nodes, does `groupby(timestamp).sum()` over nodes for `ps0` and for `ps1` and adds them. Empirical check: median per-job mean power scales linearly with `num_nodes_alloc` (1 node 690 W, 2 nodes 1,398 W, 4: 2,678 W, 8: 5,070 W, 16: 9,943 W, 32: 18,875 W; about 700 W per node). So `power_w` = mean of the series, with NO multiplication by nodes. All 231,238 jobs have a series (none empty). The mean was computed batch by batch (4,000 rows per batch). `energy_kwh` = power_w * r_act_h / 1000 (derived; PM100 has no measured energy).
